\section{Conclusions}

The STME-RS-A framework introduces a controlled anharmonic deformation of the
Randall--Sundrum geometry, producing a non-equidistant Kaluza--Klein (KK) mass
spectrum with experimentally resolvable signatures. The quartic bulk term
$\lambda_4$ modifies the Sturm--Liouville eigenvalue problem, shifts KK masses,
and enhances IR localization of wavefunctions, thereby altering couplings and
branching fractions.

We have derived the full KK spectrum, computed analytic and numerical mass
eigenvalues, and constructed benchmark points BP1--BP4 suitable for collider
searches. The resulting resonance patterns differ significantly from those
predicted by RS models, providing a clear experimental discriminator.

Collider analyses show that the HL-LHC can discover the first KK mode for all
benchmark points and can exclude the first two modes across the parameter space.
The FCC-hh extends reach to the full KK tower, enabling a definitive test of the
STME-RS-A framework.

The anharmonic KK spectrum represents a falsifiable geometric signature of
extra-dimensional physics. If observed, it would constitute direct evidence for
warped extra dimensions and provide a geometric solution to the hierarchy
problem. The STME-RS-A model therefore offers a compelling and testable extension
of the RS paradigm.